114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Imagine you want to buy 1000 cars. Not one. Not ten. One thousand.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (You can't just walk into a dealership and say "I'll take 1000 cars please" Why not? Because the dealership might have 20 cars on the lot. Maybe 50 if they're huge.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (The moment you say you need 1000 cars, what happens?)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (The price goes up.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Everyone knows you NEED to buy. Everyone knows you're desperate. The guy with 5 cars suddenly wants double the price. The guy with 10 cars wants triple. Supply and demand kicks in, and you're screwed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (So what do you do instead?)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (You buy quietly. 5 here, 10 there. You wait for people who NEED to sell, maybe they're moving, maybe they need cash, maybe they're desperate. You buy from them at a discount. You accumulate slowly, without anyone noticing.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Big players in the market do exactly the same thing.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (A hedge fund wants to buy 10,000 contracts of Gold. They can't just click "buy" and get filled. There isn't enough liquidity at one price to fill that order. If they tried, the price would spike against them and they'd get a terrible average entry.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (So they need sellers. Lots of them. And they need them to sell at a price that's favorable for the buyer.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
